  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show low\npressure over eastern Iowa and a warm front extending east across\ncentral Illinois. Thunderstorm activity is occurring just north of\nthe warm front, while further north, weaker lift originating from\nmid-level convergence is producing areas of light rain and \ndrizzle from central Wisconsin to Door County. Further north, \nnorthern stream energy is pushing a cold front south across \nnorthern Minnesota and Lake Superior. An area of clearing is \noccurring between the cold front and low clouds over northeast WI\nwhere patchy fog is occurring, dropping visibilities below 1 mile\nin spots. The reductions in visibility should be relatively \nbrief. As the cold front drops south across the region, ceiling \ntrends, gusty winds, and hazardous marine conditions are the focus\nof this forecast.\n\nThe cold front will continue to drop south across northern WI this\nmorning and central WI this afternoon. The front will bring a band\nof broken cloud cover, augmented by moisture fluxes via north \nwinds off Lake Superior. The result will be partly to mostly \ncloudy skies for much of the day. Mixing into a surge of drier air\naloft should result in partial clearing late this afternoon into \nearly this evening. \n\nMore low clouds are expected to develop tonight as conditions\nbecome very unstable over northern Lake Michigan. Inversion\nheights under 5 kft will limit the lake effect rain potential.\nThese clouds will likely stick around into Monday. A much cooler\nairmass combined with cloud cover will result in temperatures\nseveral degrees below normal today and Monday.\n\nIn a pattern which is more typical of spring, shortwave energy\noriginating from the western CONUS trough will slowly migrate east\nfrom the central Plains to the southern Great Lakes to the east\ncoast during the upcoming work week. To the north, a Hudson Bay\nhigh pressure system will build south into the northern Great\nLakes. The result will be a very dry and stable airmass across\nthe region which will shunt rain chances far south of the region.\nWith the dry airmass comes potential for frost across the\nnorthwoods on Monday night and Tuesday night. High temperatures \nwill be undergoing a slow warming trend from the lower to middle \n60s early in the work week to the low to middle 70s by Friday and \nSaturday.",
